WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.910 --> 00:00:10.180 align:start position:0%
 
[Music]  you'll be forgiven for not recognizing  Assassin's Creed as it exists today I  myself played every game since the  beginning and then about two hours of  unity before I fell off I only came back  to the series once the yearly release  schedule had been ditched playing  origins felt like returning to my  hometown to find that everything had  changed in place of a historical  playground focused on social stealth I  found an action game centered around  progression and loot upgrades that's not  to say I disliked origins in fact it's  one of my favorite entries in the series  I loved the world its characters and its  balance between the old and the new I  also loved its restrain but within the  size of its map and its willingness to  not smother me with its activities but  when compared to its predecessor  syndicate the difference is night and  day origins almost completely abandons  the idea of social stealth you are no  longer blending into crowds hiding in  plain sight or causing commotions to  slip on notice into enemy territory as  many fans have pointed out getting rid  of that social self is a pretty big deal  it's what made the series feel so fresh  in that trailer 12 years ago and I find  it ironic that a series so focused on  the exploration of DNA that it seems to  have forgotten its own in a GDC talk  earlier this year  Jean Gaston creative director of AC for  Black Flag and origins explored the  franchise's evolution throughout the  decade he recalled the creation of the  first game when developers behind prince  of persia sands of time were given two  mandates to prepare for the next console  generation and to reinvent the  action-adventure genre to do both the  team would focus on three pillars combat  navigation and social stealth if you  played any of the Assassin's Creed games  or at least any of the AC games before  origins you can see these three tenets  in abundance  the brutal close quarters combat the  fluid and stylish parkour the act of  using crowds as camouflage emerging only  to kill before vanishing again right  under your opponent's nose as each new  AC released in each new historical  setting it was these three tenets that  tie the titles together of course  there's also humane  lore of the series and it's so obvious  that Gaston didn't even need to include  it in this presentation it's the  historical settings in a recent  interview with The Guardian Jade Raymond  head of EA motive founder of Ubisoft  Toronto and producer of early Assassin's  Creed games called the historical  settings sandboxes for creatives by  establishing a foundation and a  fictional justification for these times  and places  Ubisoft was creating a sustainable  ecosystem new developers could rise up  prove themselves put their mark on the  franchise and keep the series fresh this  led to innovation at a steady pace you  can look at each game in the series and  clearly remember its flagship feature  ac2 had the mission variety that ac1 was  lacking ac3 introduced naval combat  which is pretty prevalent in a series  now ac syndicate gave us dual  protagonists and the fan-favorite  rope launcher unity for all of its flaws  paved the way for better navigation and  added co-op to the main storyline even  the fantastic and often overlooked rogue  reversed the roles of Templar and  assassin turning you from the hunter  into the hunted  rogue also told one of the best stories  in the entire series but that's another  video so I'll leave that claim  unsubstantiated here and entirely up to  my own opinion  [Music]  [Music]  all-in-all Ubisoft set out to create a  franchise and instead stumbled upon a  global phenomenon it now comprises  comics graphic novels short films a  feature-length movie and numerous  spin-off games since 2007 only two years  have passed without a mainline  Assassin's Creed entry it became so  pervasive that many felt that had grown  stale by the time syndicate rolled  around with its two playable characters  and an engrossing Victorian London  setting it kind of felt as if we had  seen it all leaving aside the possible  time periods Ubisoft might still explore  it seemed like the well was about to run  dry  Ubisoft CEO Eve game all cited several  reasons for the lack of an assassin's  Creed in 2016 one was syndicates  relatively disappointing sales another  was the need to develop new engines as  the foundations for future games and  although guillermo himself didn't  mention it various reports about  development crunch and long working  hours leaked from various levels of  Ubisoft Studios so for one reason or  another it was time to give Assassin's  Creed a break if only for a little while  by the time we first glimpsed Assassin's  Creed origins it had been in development  for three years  several Ubisoft Studios led by UB  Montreal revealed origins at e3 2017  showing the mega byuk in his Egyptian  setting this also confirmed an earlier  leak from kotaku when origins was still  known by its codename Empire the trailer  also showed a new style of combat  swapping the animation focused melees of  earlier games for a system based on  hitboxes and deliberate timing as more  news poured out from e3 it became clear  that origins was also heavily influenced  by RPG progression with a full-fledged  color-coded loot system where a knock  four by x hood his hidden blade or the  assassins iconic leap of faith it could  have been a whole new series like I said  before I love origins I love its  characters its inhabitants its vast  stretches of empty desert I love its  ability to let me find my own story but  also the confidence to tell its own when  need be I also respect the hell out of  its writers for tackling one of the most  frequently fan or  busted settings and for knocking it out  of the park it was a technological jump  to be sure but also a cultural one and  it represented a major shift in what the  developers wanted Assassin's Creed to be  in this way origins was a major turning  point and as of now I'm worried about  the series new direction Assassin's  Creed Odyssey is one of my favorite  games this year and I had a blast  sinking seventy five hours into its  massive Greek archipelago but still it  feels as if you be soft emphasized the  wrong aspects from a sea origins its new  identity as one of hundreds of side  quests numerous overlapping systems and  a loot fest that rivals that of destiny  or diablo years ago assassin's creed was  setting precedents for open-world design  now despite the fun i'm having with a  series it feels as if it's playing  catch-up  I don't wanna complain about an  abundance of content and I sure as hell  don't wanna complain about having fun  but still at a certain point I have to  wonder if you be soft strayed too far  I'm almost always of the opinion that  developers should evolve whenever  possible it brings a wider spectrum of  games and experiences to the table and  allows for fresh ideas to enter the ring  at a regular pace but I can also  empathize with the people who feel as if  Assassin's Creed is absent from the  modern video game landscape social  stealth is almost completely gone the  map feels more about size than substance  and the franchise feels built around its  progression rather than its gameplay  this can still be fun and I have had a  ton of it but at what point does  Assassin's Creed lose its identity  at its core Assassin's Creed's plot is  one of freedom versus control on the one  hand you have the Templars an ancient  order hell-bent on maintaining order  through power and influence the  assassins on the other hand represent  the opposite complete and utter freedom  even if it means overwhelming chaos the  story of the franchise's evolution is  the exact same one freedom versus  control and with all of its fourth wall  breaking and self-aware storytelling I  can't imagine this irony is lost on the  developers they themselves are  constantly grappling with that same  dilemma trying to find that balance  between the control and order of earlier  games and the player freedom of recent  ones I think there is a balance to  strike and it is fascinating watching  them trying to  I get Assassin's Creed as it stands  today is the result of a 10-year search  for an identity in fact you could argue  that it's that very search itself that  has come to define the series I have no  clue where that next 10 years will bring  us but I think one thing is for certain  that journey will be entertaining

